===== FSOPS REACHABILITY (basic read/write/list as CLI verbs?) =====
--- toolsd read (expected: not a CLI verb)
$ toolsd read
toolsd: unknown subcommand "read"

Usage: toolsd <subcommand> [args]

toolsd is the coding-hermes-tools CLI: agent-safe code modification primitives
(strict patch apply, diff3 merge, atomic multi-file transactions). Every verb
describes itself: what it does, WHEN to reach for it, its knobs, its examples
and its outcomes.

Subcommands:
  version    Print the toolsd version and exit.
  help       Print the surface, or one verb's full descriptor: toolsd help <verb>.
  describe   Print the whole tool surface: every descriptor in full (--json for the registry verbatim).
  config     Print every setting's effective value and which layer set it (flag, environment, file, default).
  metrics    Count the toolkit's real usage from the invocation ledger: what was chosen, and whether it worked.
  status     One monitoring screen: the usage counters, the invocation log tail, and the resolved settings.
  diff3      Three-way merge two edited versions against a common base.
  patch      Parse and strictly apply unified diffs.
  apply      Atomically apply edits across multiple files (all-or-nothing).
  replace    Replace literal text in one file in place (unique match, or --all).
  lease      Advisory edit-lease registry: acquire, renew, release, status.
  session    Git worktree sessions: start, end, list.
  lsp        Language-server queries: definition, references, check.
  narrate    Turn a unified diff into a JSON or Markdown evidence block.
  probe      Run an HTTP probe battery against one endpoint (N requests, bounded concurrency, rate cap).
  mcp        Serve every verb as an MCP tool on stdio (newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0).

  Run `toolsd <subcommand> --help` (or `toolsd help <subcommand>`) for one
  verb's full descriptor.

Library primitives (Go packages, no subcommand):
  fsops      Confined file-system operations: rooted read, whole-content write, mkdir, list.

The whole surface as data: `toolsd describe` (text) or `toolsd describe --json`.
rc=2
